<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hanukkah</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chanukah, Festival of Lights</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hanukkah is an eight-day Jewish festival beginning on 25 Kislev, usually in December. It commemorates the rededication of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple in Jerusalem</a> in 164 BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judas-maccabeus">Judas Maccabeus</a> and his followers, after the Seleucid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iv-epiphanes">Antiochus IV Epiphanes</a> had desecrated it. The festival is marked by lighting one more candle or lamp each night on a nine-branched candelabrum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hanukkah.jpg" alt="Hanukkah" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: YB13D · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hanukkah_%D7%97%D7%92_%D7%97%D7%A0%D7%95%D7%9B%D7%94.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> in 323 BCE, Judea became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Kingdom</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. In 200 BCE King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> defeated Ptolemy V Epiphanes at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-panium">Battle of Panium</a>, and Judea passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>. Antiochus III guaranteed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> their ancestral customs and the practice of their religion in the Temple. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iv-epiphanes">Antiochus IV Epiphanes</a> reversed this policy, apparently after a dispute over the leadership of the Temple and the office of High Priest.</p><p>Some modern scholars, following the Second Book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maccabees">Maccabees</a>, see the king as intervening in a civil war between traditionalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and a Hellenizing faction. These groups contested the office of High Priest, with figures bearing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> names such as Onias set against Hellenizing priests with Greek names such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jason">Jason</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelaus">Menelaus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In the traditional account, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> was looted and its services stopped, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> was outlawed. In 167 BCE Antiochus IV ordered an altar to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> set up in the Temple, banned circumcision and had pigs sacrificed on the altar. The priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mattathias">Mattathias</a> and his five sons launched a revolt. After Mattathias died, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judas-maccabeus">Judas Maccabeus</a>, took command and won campaigns against Seleucid forces.</p><p>In 164 BCE the rebels recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a>, which was cleansed and rededicated. The Jewish historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>, in his Jewish Antiquities, says that Judas ordered yearly eight-day festivities, which Josephus calls the festival of Lights. The story is told in the First and Second Books of Maccabees, held canonical by the Roman Catholic and Orthodox Churches but not part of the Masoretic Text.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Hanukkah begins on 25 Kislev and lasts eight nights. Each night a light is added to the nine-branched menorah, the extra light, the shamash, being used to kindle the others. Customs include the hymn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maoz-tzur">Ma'oz Tzur</a>, the game of dreidel, gifts of money called gelt, and fried foods such as latkes and jam-filled doughnuts, recalling the tradition of a small flask of oil that kept the Temple's menorah alight for eight days.</p><p>The festival is of rabbinic origin and, in religious terms, relatively minor. It grew in cultural weight under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zionism">Zionism</a>, which stressed the Hasmonean victory, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, where from the nineteenth century Reform leaders such as Isaac Mayer Wise made it a children's celebration. Because it often falls near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a>, it also took on a role as a Jewish counterpart to the season's festivities.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hanukkah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…with the Maccabean revolt in Judaea (167–142 BCE), triggered by Antiochus IV Epiphanes's desecration of the Jerusalem Temple and attempted suppression of Jewish religious practice, produced the independent Hasmonean state, commemorated in the festival of <b>Hanukkah</b>, whose politics and religion formed the world into which Christianity was born.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…Atonement, a day of fasting and repentance, fall in the autumn; the pilgrimage festivals of Passover, which recalls the exodus from Egypt, Shavuot, which celebrates the giving of the Torah, and Sukkot, the festival of booths, recall the journey of the Israelites; <b>Hanukkah</b> and Purim remember later deliverances.</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hanukkah</b>, the eight-day festival of Judaism that begins on 25 Kislev, usually in December, commemorates an event: the rededication of the Temple in Jerusalem in 164 BCE by Judas Maccabeus and his followers, after the Seleucid king Antiochus IV Epiphanes had desecrated it.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li><li><blockquote>Because of its simple metre the hymn can be sung to almost any tune: congregations use seasonal ones, such as Ma'oz Tzur before <b>Hanukkah</b>, and Hebrew schools and summer camps sing it for fun to &quot;Yankee Doodle&quot; or &quot;Jamaica Farewell&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/adon-olam">Adon Olam</a></li><li><blockquote>…six psalms with a blessing before and after, is recited on the first days of Passover, on Shavuot, on all seven days of Sukkot, on Shemini Atzeret and Simchat Torah, and on the eight days of <b>Hanukkah</b>; the sages gave a short word, &quot;BeBeTaCh&quot;, for remembering the days.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hallel">Hallel</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Ma'oz Tzur&quot; is a piyyut, a Hebrew liturgical poem, sung on the festival of <b>Hanukkah</b> after the festival lights are lit.</blockquote><a href="/songs/maoz-tzur">Ma'oz Tzur</a></li></ul></section>
